.TH CLIPNEST 1 "2026" "clipnest" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clipnest \- clipboard history, snippets, and on-device OCR
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clipnest
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B clipnest-ctl
\fICOMMAND\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Clipnest watches the clipboard, keeps a searchable history, lets you save
reusable text snippets, and (with the optional
.B clipnest-ocr
package installed) recognizes text in copied images entirely on-device \-
no network access is ever used for this or anything else Clipnest does.
.PP
Clipnest is normally started automatically over D-Bus activation (see
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/services/app.clipnest.Clipnest.service
and
.IR /usr/share/applications/app.clipnest.Clipnest.desktop )
the first time something calls it \- e.g. its own autostart entry, or a
global shortcut. Running
.B clipnest
directly is only needed for debugging.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-gapplication-service
Run as the background D-Bus service instance rather than opening a window
immediately. This is how autostart and D-Bus activation both invoke it.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the installed version and exit.
.SH CLIPNEST-CTL
.B clipnest-ctl
is a thin command-line wrapper around the running instance's D-Bus
interface, for scripting global actions without a keyboard shortcut:
.TP
.B clipnest-ctl toggle-picker
Open or close the clipboard-history picker.
.TP
.B clipnest-ctl expand-snippet
Expand the current selection as a snippet keyword.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/clipnest/settings.json
Per-user settings (capture on/off, history retention, excluded apps, OCR
toggle/quality, update-check preference). Plain JSON, XDG Base Directory
Specification location (falls back to
.I ~/.config
if
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/clipnest/
Per-user clipboard history database and blob storage (large payloads \-
images, rich text \- content-addressed by hash).
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/clipnest.desktop
Present only if you have enabled "Launch at login" in Clipnest's own
Settings. Clipnest manages this file itself; it is not installed by the
.B clipnest
package.
.SH UNINSTALLING
Removing the
.B clipnest
package (e.g.
.IR "apt remove clipnest" )
does not delete your clipboard history or settings \- dpkg has no
supported mechanism to reach into a per-user home directory during
package removal, and a system package purge must not destroy personal
data that never belonged to the package in the first place. Purging the
package (\fIapt purge clipnest\fR) removes the
.B clipnest-input
system group (see
.BR clipnest-grant-input (8))
and recompiles system caches, but still leaves your data in place.
.PP
To remove your data as well, once you are sure you want to, run for each
user who has used Clipnest:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rm -rf ~/.config/clipnest ~/.local/share/clipnest
.fi
.RE
.PP
If you also ran the auto-paste opt-in (see
.BR clipnest-grant-input (8)),
you may want to remove yourself from the
.B clipnest-input
group as well:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo gpasswd -d "$USER" clipnest-input
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clipnest-grant-input (8),
.BR clipnest-ctl (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Aayush Gour
